`width` and `height` on a canvas are the coordinate space the shapes were
placed in, not the size it is shown at. `scalingMode` is what happens to the
picture when the room it is given is not that size.

This is the source that names no mode. `center` is the default and adds
nothing: the canvas stays the size of its own drawing, redrawn at that size,
which is what every canvas did before there was a choice. That is the point of
the archive -- it is the baseline the scaled forms in
canvas_scaling_mode_fit.txtar and canvas_scaling_mode_stretch.txtar are read
against, and the denies below are the machinery those two add and this one must
not.

html and gtk4 still pass the mode, as the empty string, so the choice reaches
the same argument either way rather than through a second code path. fyne and
Compose pick a different construct instead -- an Image and a fixed `size`
modifier -- because for them the unscaled case is the cheaper one.

deny out/none/html/index.html `aspect-ratio` -- the element keeps the drawing's own size, so no box shape is reserved for it to scale into
deny out/none/html/index.html `_snglCanvasWatch\(\$0,` -- an unscaled canvas is drawn once; nothing follows the layout box, so a resize has nothing to redraw
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `canvas.NewRaster` -- with nothing to scale the canvas stays the cheaper Image, rasterised once rather than regenerated per redraw
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `gtk_widget_set_hexpand` -- the drawing area takes its natural size, so it does not claim the extra room a scaled canvas needs

-- center.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

window #w {
    draw.canvas(width=200px, height=100px) {
        draw.rect(x=0.0, y=0.0, w=200.0, h=100.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=#3366cc})
    }
}
